        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC9908054/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>In the HUNT Study, all residents aged ≥20 years in the Nord-Trøndelag region, Norway, have been invited to repeated surveys since 1984-86.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000396 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000396">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90709872</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A US cohort, launched by the Simons Foundation Autism Research Initiative (SFARI), of individuals living in the United States with a professional diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder together with their parents and an unaffected sibling, recruited nationwide through 21 clinical sites and social media and taking part remotely online, with questionnaires and saliva samples for genetic analysis (exome sequencing and genome-wide genotyping, later also whole-genome sequencing). It collects genomic, environmental and longitudinal behavioural and medical data, returns genetic results to participants, and recontacts them and connects them with researchers for new studies; it enrolled 18,089 individuals with autism and 28,515 of their family members in its first 12 months of recruitment, to May 2017.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPARK</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Simons Foundation Powering Autism Research</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Simons Foundation Powering Autism Research for Knowledge</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000396"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90709872</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Human and bacterial genetic variation shape oral microbiomes and health.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:41606319</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12979206/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To create a dataset suitable for exploring variation in the oral microbiome and the way it is shaped by human genetic variation, we analysed DNA sequencing reads previously generated from whole-genome sequencing (WGS) of saliva samples from 12,519 participants in the Simons Foundation Powering Autism Research (SPARK) cohort25 (Fig. 1a), building on previous work26,27.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000396"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A US cohort, launched by the Simons Foundation Autism Research Initiative (SFARI), of individuals living in the United States with a professional diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder together with their parents and an unaffected sibling, recruited nationwide through 21 clinical sites and social media and taking part remotely online, with questionnaires and saliva samples for genetic analysis (exome sequencing and genome-wide genotyping, later also whole-genome sequencing). It collects genomic, environmental and longitudinal behavioural and medical data, returns genetic results to participants, and recontacts them and connects them with researchers for new studies; it enrolled 18,089 individuals with autism and 28,515 of their family members in its first 12 months of recruitment, to May 2017.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:29420931</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:41606319</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000396"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPARK</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000396"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC7444276/unicode</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Any individual living in the United States with a professional diagnosis (by a physician, psychologist, or therapist) of ASD plus their parents and an unaffected sibling are eligible to participate in SPARK.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000397 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000397">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90257106</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multicentre prospective observational cohort of 5,499 racially and ethnically diverse adults with chronic kidney disease, recruited at seven clinical centres in the United States in two phases, 2003–2008 (adults aged 21 to 74 with an estimated glomerular filtration rate of 20–70 mL/min/1.73 m²) and 2013–2015 (adults aged 45 to 79 with more preserved kidney function, eGFR 45–70), and followed with annual clinic visits, six-monthly telephone contacts and annual blood and urine collection to examine risk factors for the progression of chronic kidney disease and cardiovascular disease.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CRIC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CRIC Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Chronic Renal Insufficiency Cohort</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Chronic Renal Insufficiency Cohort Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000397"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90257106</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Trans-ethnic genome-wide association study of blood metabolites in the Chronic Renal Insufficiency Cohort (CRIC) study.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:35120996</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A35120996+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Here, we conducted a GWAS of 537 blood metabolites measured in the Chronic Renal Insufficiency Cohort (CRIC) Study, with separate analyses in 822 White and 687 Black study participants.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000397"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A multicentre prospective observational cohort of 5,499 racially and ethnically diverse adults with chronic kidney disease, recruited at seven clinical centres in the United States in two phases, 2003–2008 (adults aged 21 to 74 with an estimated glomerular filtration rate of 20–70 mL/min/1.73 m²) and 2013–2015 (adults aged 45 to 79 with more preserved kidney function, eGFR 45–70), and followed with annual clinic visits, six-monthly telephone contacts and annual blood and urine collection to examine risk factors for the progression of chronic kidney disease and cardiovascular disease.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:12819321</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:35175342</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:42318085</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000397"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CRIC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000397"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A33177074+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Chronic Renal Insufficiency Cohort (CRIC) Study is an ongoing, multicenter, longitudinal study of nearly 5500 adults with CKD in the United States.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


