    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000439 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000439">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90095485</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A prospective cross-sectional cohort of about 1,500 adults with Crohn&apos;s disease or ulcerative colitis and non-IBD controls (people undergoing colon cancer screening), recruited between December 2013 and September 2016 during their endoscopy visits to the outpatient and endoscopy units of the Mount Sinai Hospital, with paired intestinal biopsies and blood samples, RNA sequencing, genotype data and clinical, endoscopic and histological assessments for molecular studies of inflammatory bowel disease.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MSCCR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mount Sinai Crohn&apos;s and Colitis Registry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000439"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90095485</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Integrative Analysis of the Inflammatory Bowel Disease Serum Metabolome Improves Our Understanding of Genetic Etiology and Points to Novel Putative Therapeutic Targets.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:34780722</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC9214725/unicode</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Patients with UC (n=484) or CD (n=464) and HCs (ages 18+, n=365) were recruited as part of a cross-sectional study, the Mount Sinai Crohn’s and Colitis Registry (MSCCR), between December 2013 and September 2016 from the outpatient and endoscopy units of the Mount Sinai Hospital.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
