    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000848"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90909152</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Polygenic Scores of Cardiometabolic Risk Factors in American Indian Adults.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:40072435</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A40072435+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Strong Heart Study (SHS) is a large American Indian cohort recruited from 1989 to 1991, with ongoing follow-up (phase VII).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000848"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A cohort of 4,549 self-identified American Indian adults aged 45 years or older, recruited in 1989–1991 from tribal communities in Arizona, Oklahoma, North Dakota and South Dakota, United States, and followed through repeated examinations to estimate cardiovascular disease rates and risk factors in American Indians.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:40072435</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000848"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SHS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000848"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11904716/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>This genetic association study used data from the SHS, a large, well-characterized cohort of 4549 self-identified American Indian adults aged 45 years or older, who were recruited from tribal communities in Arizona, Oklahoma, North Dakota, and South Dakota (1989-1991).9 The SHS cohort has completed 3 clinical examinations (phase I, 1989-1991; phase II, 1993-1995; and phase III, 1998-1999) and has collected comprehensive data on clinical, lifestyle, and physical measures, as well as medications at each clinical visit, using standardized protocols.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000849 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000849">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90188370</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A stroke study in which 1,200 black and 1,200 white stroke patients were prospectively recruited between 1999 and 2010 from three hospitals covering a contiguous area of South London, United Kingdom, with black African and African Caribbean community controls from general practice lists in the same catchment area (1999–2012), to study stroke subtypes and the genetic and other risk factors for stroke in African and African Caribbean people.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SLESS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">South London Ethnicity and Stroke Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000849"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90188370</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Contribution of Common Genetic Variants to Risk of Early-Onset Ischemic Stroke.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:36240095</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://cdn-links.lww.com/permalink/wnl/c/wnl_2022_08_08_mitchell_1_sdc1.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The South London Ethnicity and Stroke Study (SLESS) is a prospective study begun in 1999 that has recruited consecutive black patients with stroke from a contiguous catchment area covered by 3 hospitals in South London</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000849"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A stroke study in which 1,200 black and 1,200 white stroke patients were prospectively recruited between 1999 and 2010 from three hospitals covering a contiguous area of South London, United Kingdom, with black African and African Caribbean community controls from general practice lists in the same catchment area (1999–2012), to study stroke subtypes and the genetic and other risk factors for stroke in African and African Caribbean people.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:27197724</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:28349126</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000849"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SLESS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000849"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A27197724+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Exploring the differences in aetiological stroke subtypes, and the extent to which they are explained by conventional and novel risk factors, is an important step in elucidating the underlying mechanisms for this increased stroke risk.MethodsBetween 1999 and 2010, 1200 black and 1200 white stroke patients were prospectively recruited from a contiguous geographical area in South London in the UK.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


