        <owl:annotatedTarget>A two-generation, family-based study in the Saguenay–Lac-Saint-Jean region of Quebec, Canada, a genetic founder population, of adolescent sibships aged 12–18 of French-Canadian origin recruited through high schools in 2003–2012, half of them exposed prenatally to maternal cigarette smoking and matched with non-exposed adolescents for maternal education and school, and of their parents (partly assessed in 2003–2012 and fully in 2012–2015), with extensive phenotyping including brain and abdominal MRI, cardiometabolic assessments and genome-wide genotyping, to investigate the aetiology, early stages and trans-generational trajectories of common cardiometabolic and brain diseases.</owl:annotatedTarget>
