    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001385 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001385">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Greece"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Israel"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Netherlands"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90833053</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A consortium formed in 2015 around the New York Genome Center, through which 41 ALS clinics and research institutions in the United States, the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, Greece and Israel contribute blood and post-mortem brain and spinal cord tissue from people with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, other neurological diseases and non-neurological controls. The samples are whole-genome and RNA sequenced at the New York Genome Center and their clinical data harmonised into an openly accessible genomic resource for ALS research.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NYGC ALS Consortium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NYGC ALS consortium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NYG_ALS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>The New York Genome Center&apos;s ALS Consortium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">New York Genome Center ALS Consortium</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001385"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90833053</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Large-scale exome analyses reveal new rare variant contributions in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:41917433</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13083253/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Case cohorts included the Project MinE ALS sequencing consortium (7,614 cases; 2,605 controls)47, the NYGC ALS Consortium (2,650 cases; 342 controls), the ALS Sequencing Consortium (2,851 cases)5, two cohorts from the FALS consortium (1,277 cases; phs001585), the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Exome Sequencing of FALS Project (194 cases; phs000101), two Australian cohorts described in ref. 48 (125 cases, 18 controls) and ref. 49 (568 cases), and a Chinese motor neurone disease (MND) cohort50 (583 cases, 182 controls).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001385"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A consortium formed in 2015 around the New York Genome Center, through which 41 ALS clinics and research institutions in the United States, the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, Greece and Israel contribute blood and post-mortem brain and spinal cord tissue from people with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, other neurological diseases and non-neurological controls. The samples are whole-genome and RNA sequenced at the New York Genome Center and their clinical data harmonised into an openly accessible genomic resource for ALS research.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.04.29.26350889v1.full-text</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001385"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>NYG_ALS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001385"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Israel"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.04.29.26350889v1.full-text</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Blood and autopsy tissue samples were obtained from 41 ALS clinics and research institutions spanning the USA, the UK, the Netherlands, Greece, and Israel (Fig. 1A).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001386 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001386">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/New_Zealand"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Oceania"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90432152</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A case-control study of abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) in New Zealand. The Vascular Research Consortium of New Zealand recruited men and women with AAA (infrarenal aortic diameter of at least 30 mm on ultrasound or CT scan, about 80% after surgical repair), nearly all of Anglo-European ancestry, and elderly Anglo-European controls in whom AAA was excluded by abdominal ultrasound and who were also screened for peripheral and carotid artery disease. Its participants were genotyped in two genome-wide case-control sets for genetic studies of AAA.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NZ</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>NZ-VAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>New Zealand (NZ) Vascular Genetics Study AAA GWAS dataset</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>New Zealand Vascular Genetic Study AAA GWAS dataset</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">NZ AAA Genetics Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001386"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90432152</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association meta-analysis identifies risk loci for abdominal aortic aneurysm and highlights PCSK9 as a therapeutic target.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:37845353</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10632148/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>These 14 discovery cohorts are as follows: (1) Atherosclerosis Risk in Communities (ARIC); (2) Copenhagen Hospital Biobank—Cardiovascular Disease Cohort and Danish Blood Donor Study (CHB-CVDC+DBDS); (3) Cardiovascular Health Improvement Project and Michigan Genomics Initiative (CHIP + MGI); (4) deCODE; (5) DiscovEHR; (6) Electronic Medical Records and Genomics (eMERGE); (7) Trøndelag Health study (HUNT), (8) Mayo Vascular Disease Biorepository (Mayo VDB); (9) MVP; (10) NZ AAA Genetics Study; (11) Penn Medicine biobank (PMBB); (12) Triple A Barcelona Study (TABS); (13) UKAGS and Viborg Vascular (UKAGS+VIVA); (14) UKBB (Supplementary Fig. 16).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001386"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A case-control study of abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) in New Zealand. The Vascular Research Consortium of New Zealand recruited men and women with AAA (infrarenal aortic diameter of at least 30 mm on ultrasound or CT scan, about 80% after surgical repair), nearly all of Anglo-European ancestry, and elderly Anglo-European controls in whom AAA was excluded by abdominal ultrasound and who were also screened for peripheral and carotid artery disease. Its participants were genotyped in two genome-wide case-control sets for genetic studies of AAA.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:27899403</obo:IAO_0000119>
