    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001407 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001407">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Chile"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/SouthAmerica"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90502757</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cohort of healthy, full-term infants from low- to middle-income neighbourhoods of Santiago, Chile, recruited through community clinics in 1991–1996 for an iron-deficiency anaemia preventive trial or a neuromaturation study, and followed up through childhood, adolescence and adulthood in studies of nutrition, development, substance use and cardiometabolic risk.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SLS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Santiago Longitudinal Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001407"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90502757</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Trans-ancestry genome-wide association study of childhood body mass index identifies novel loci and age-specific effects.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39885687</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A39885687+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To identify genetic loci associated with early-childhood body mass index (BMI), we performed GWAS of BMI Z scores in eight ancestrally diverse cohorts: ZOE 2.0 cohort, the Santiago Longitudinal Study (SLS), the Vanderbilt University BioVU biobank, the Geisinger MyCode Health Initiative biobank, Study of Latino (SOL) Youth, Pelotas (Brazil) Birth Cohort, Cameron County Hispanic Cohort (CCHC), and Viva La Familia cohort.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001407"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A cohort of healthy, full-term infants from low- to middle-income neighbourhoods of Santiago, Chile, recruited through community clinics in 1991–1996 for an iron-deficiency anaemia preventive trial or a neuromaturation study, and followed up through childhood, adolescence and adulthood in studies of nutrition, development, substance use and cardiometabolic risk.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:24662965</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:32499257</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:34556688</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:35501766</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:39885687</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001407"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SLS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001407"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Chile"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11875162/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Santiago Longitudinal Study (SLS) is a cohort of participants recruited from community clinics in Santiago, Chile, at infancy between 1991 and 1996 for participation in a randomized trial of iron supplementation to prevent iron deficiency anemia, as described previously.24 Participants were followed during infancy and at ages 5, 10, 16 or 17, and 21 or 22 years and assessed for a range of outcomes.25 Standardized BMIzs were computed using the 2000 CDC Growth Reference charts.21 A total of n = 861 SLS participants were included in the GWAS of BMIz (mean age = 5.51 years).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000001"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/China"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Eastern_Asia"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90132306</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The Shanghai Women&apos;s Health Study and the Shanghai Men&apos;s Health Study taken together: two population-based prospective cohorts of middle-aged and elderly Chinese adults in urban Shanghai, China, which recruited women in 1996–2000 and men in 2002–2006 and whose data and biospecimens are often analysed jointly, for example in nested case–control and genome-wide association studies.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SMWHS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SWHS/SMHS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Shanghai Mens and Shanghai Womens Health Studies</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Shanghai Women&apos;s and Men&apos;s Health Studies</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90132306</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genetic Architecture of Plasma Alpha-Aminoadipic Acid Reveals a Relationship With High-Density Lipoprotein Cholesterol.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:35621206</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A35621206+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>We identified genetic determinants of plasma 2-AAA through meta-analysis of genome-wide association study data in 5456 individuals of European, African, and Asian ancestry from the Framingham Heart Study, Diabetes Prevention Program, Jackson Heart Study, and Shanghai Women&apos;s and Men&apos;s Health Studies.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The Shanghai Women&apos;s Health Study and the Shanghai Men&apos;s Health Study taken together: two population-based prospective cohorts of middle-aged and elderly Chinese adults in urban Shanghai, China, which recruited women in 1996–2000 and men in 2002–2006 and whose data and biospecimens are often analysed jointly, for example in nested case–control and genome-wide association studies.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:28135196</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:35621206</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:40576378</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SMWHS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SWHS/SMHS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001408"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/China"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC9238724/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The SWHS (Shanghai Women’s Health Study) and the SMHS (Shanghai Men’s Health Study) are ongoing prospective cohort studies that recruited 75 000 women and 61 500 men of Chinese ancestry, aged 35 to 75 years, from Shanghai, China.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001409 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001409">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
