    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001413 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001413">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Singapore"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/South-Eastern_Asia"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90454282</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A preconception, longitudinal cohort of Chinese, Malay or Indian women aged 18–45 who intended to conceive and deliver in Singapore, recruited in February 2015–October 2017 through a preconception clinic at KK Women&apos;s and Children&apos;s Hospital and through public outreach, and followed with their children from before conception through pregnancy into early childhood. Its aim is to study how nutrition, lifestyle and maternal mood before and during pregnancy affect the offspring&apos;s epigenome, duration of gestation, fetal growth, and metabolic and neural phenotypes.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>S-PRESTO</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Singapore PREconception Study of long-Term maternal and child Outcomes</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Singapore Preconception Study of Long-Term Maternal and Child Outcomes</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90454282</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Exploring multi-omics and clinical characteristics linked to accelerated biological aging in Asian women of reproductive age: insights from the S-PRESTO study.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39516835</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11549770/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Singapore Preconception Study of Long-Term Maternal and Child Outcomes (S-PRESTO) longitudinal preconception cohort, a deeply-phenotyped, multi-ethnic cohort of Asian women of reproductive age, provides a promising opportunity to address these research gaps.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A preconception, longitudinal cohort of Chinese, Malay or Indian women aged 18–45 who intended to conceive and deliver in Singapore, recruited in February 2015–October 2017 through a preconception clinic at KK Women&apos;s and Children&apos;s Hospital and through public outreach, and followed with their children from before conception through pregnancy into early childhood. Its aim is to study how nutrition, lifestyle and maternal mood before and during pregnancy affect the offspring&apos;s epigenome, duration of gestation, fetal growth, and metabolic and neural phenotypes.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:33222050</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>S-PRESTO</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Singapore"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A33222050+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Between February 2015 and October 2017, the S-PRESTO study recruited 1039 Chinese, Malay or Indian (or any combinations thereof) women aged 18-45 years and who intended to get pregnant and deliver in Singapore, resulting in 1032 unique participants and 373 children born in the cohort.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001414 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001414">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Switzerland"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90726424</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A nationwide, prospective, multicentre cohort of neonates and children younger than 17 with blood culture-proven bacterial sepsis meeting criteria for systemic inflammatory response syndrome, enrolled from September 2011 to December 2015 at ten tertiary and regional paediatric hospitals in Switzerland, including all five university centres and all paediatric intensive care units, to study the incidence, clinical features and outcomes of paediatric sepsis.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>SPSS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Swiss Pediatric Sepsis Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90726424</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association study of paediatric bacteraemia and sepsis.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:42241732</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A42241732+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Using samples and clinical data from 650 children enrolled in the Swiss Pediatric Sepsis Study, a national multicentre cohort for culture-proven bacterial sepsis, we conducted within-cohort analyses and a separate case-control analysis in 510 cases and 994 controls, testing genome-wide polymorphisms for association with sepsis susceptibility and, in cases only, with disease characteristics.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A nationwide, prospective, multicentre cohort of neonates and children younger than 17 with blood culture-proven bacterial sepsis meeting criteria for systemic inflammatory response syndrome, enrolled from September 2011 to December 2015 at ten tertiary and regional paediatric hospitals in Switzerland, including all five university centres and all paediatric intensive care units, to study the incidence, clinical features and outcomes of paediatric sepsis.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:30169202</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:42241732</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>SPSS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Switzerland"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13266212/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Swiss Pediatric Sepsis Study was a nationwide, prospective, multicentre cohort conducted from September 1, 2011, to December 31, 2015, across 10 tertiary and regional paediatric hospitals in Switzerland, including all five university centres and all paediatric intensive care units.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001415 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001415">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90244683</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A long-term, multicentre study funded by the US National Institute of Mental Health that enrolled outpatients aged 15 or older with any subtype of bipolar disorder at 22 academic medical centres in the United States and followed them over seven years. All participants entered a naturalistic standard care pathway, with randomized care pathways for acute and refractory depression, to answer questions about the effectiveness of treatments for bipolar disorder.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>STEP</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>STEP-BD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Systematic Treatment Enhancement Program for Bipolar Disorder</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90244683</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Body weight changes and bipolar disorder: a molecular pathway analysis.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:36305391</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A36305391+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The STEP-BD sample is analyzed through a hypothesis-free molecular pathway analysis in order to detect the molecular pathways that distinguish individuals who experience weight change during BD treatment from those who do not.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A long-term, multicentre study funded by the US National Institute of Mental Health that enrolled outpatients aged 15 or older with any subtype of bipolar disorder at 22 academic medical centres in the United States and followed them over seven years. All participants entered a naturalistic standard care pathway, with randomized care pathways for acute and refractory depression, to answer questions about the effectiveness of treatments for bipolar disorder.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:25844303</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:35773558</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
