        <rdfs:comment>To describe the clinical, endoscopic, and histopathologic features of a large number of children and adults with EoE across the United States.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001430 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001430">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90809464</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An online study of adults aged 18 or over who speak English fluently, conducted by researchers at Vanderbilt University Medical Center in Nashville, Tennessee, and recruited worldwide, mainly through ResearchMatch and also through Vanderbilt newsletters and research mailing lists, Reddit, Facebook advertising and social media sharing. Participants completed web-based questionnaires on music engagement and auditory tasks of musical rhythm and speech perception, and those with a US mailing address were invited to return mail-in saliva samples for genotyping, to study the genetics of musicality.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Vanderbilt_OM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Vanderbilt Online Musicality Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001430"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90809464</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide investigation of prosody perception: Shared genetic influences between speech rhythm, musical rhythm, and reading traits.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:41715922</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13091348/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The study population was a subset of those who participated in the Vanderbilt Online Musicality Study,47 where participants completed various internet-based tasks, including TOPsy, and submitted genetic samples.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001430"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An online study of adults aged 18 or over who speak English fluently, conducted by researchers at Vanderbilt University Medical Center in Nashville, Tennessee, and recruited worldwide, mainly through ResearchMatch and also through Vanderbilt newsletters and research mailing lists, Reddit, Facebook advertising and social media sharing. Participants completed web-based questionnaires on music engagement and auditory tasks of musical rhythm and speech perception, and those with a US mailing address were invited to return mail-in saliva samples for genotyping, to study the genetics of musicality.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:36718543</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:41715922</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001430"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Vanderbilt_OM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001430"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10038917/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Saliva collection kits were sent to 2,286 of these participants meeting the following criteria: they had completed the entire survey, reported that their data was usable, and had a valid US mailing address.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001431 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001431">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90502757</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A family study of Hispanic children in Houston, Texas, conducted at the USDA/ARS Children&apos;s Nutrition Research Center of Baylor College of Medicine, which in 2000–2005 recruited families through a proband aged 4–19 with a BMI above the 95th percentile, together with the proband&apos;s siblings aged 4–19 and their parents. The children were phenotyped in depth (anthropometry, body composition by dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry, diet, physical activity, energy expenditure and metabolic risk factors) and followed up after one year, to map genetic and environmental contributions to childhood obesity and its comorbidities.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>VFA</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>VIVA LA FAMILIA Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Viva La Familia</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Viva La Familia Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Viva la Familia Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001431"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90502757</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Trans-ancestry genome-wide association study of childhood body mass index identifies novel loci and age-specific effects.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39885687</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11875162/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Viva La Familia study recruited n = 1,030 children from 319 families between November 2000 and August 2004 from a Hispanic population in Houston, Texas, to assess genetic and environmental factors affecting obesity and its related comorbidities.31 A total of n = 656 participants were included in GWAS analyses (mean age = 10.7 for girls and mean age = 11.2 for boys).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001431"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A family study of Hispanic children in Houston, Texas, conducted at the USDA/ARS Children&apos;s Nutrition Research Center of Baylor College of Medicine, which in 2000–2005 recruited families through a proband aged 4–19 with a BMI above the 95th percentile, together with the proband&apos;s siblings aged 4–19 and their parents. The children were phenotyped in depth (anthropometry, body composition by dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry, diet, physical activity, energy expenditure and metabolic risk factors) and followed up after one year, to map genetic and environmental contributions to childhood obesity and its comorbidities.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:16960181</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23251661</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001431"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>VFA</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001431"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11875162/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Viva La Familia study recruited n = 1,030 children from 319 families between November 2000 and August 2004 from a Hispanic population in Houston, Texas, to assess genetic and environmental factors affecting obesity and its related comorbidities.31 A total of n = 656 participants were included in GWAS analyses (mean age = 10.7 for girls and mean age = 11.2 for boys).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001432 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001432">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Oceania"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST010304</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based collection of adult melanoma cases resident in Western Australia and diagnosed between January 2006 and September 2009, identified through the Western Australian Cancer Registry and invited by mail with their diagnosing doctor&apos;s consent, with registry pathology data, a telephone questionnaire on melanoma risk factors such as sun exposure and skin type, and blood samples for DNA, RNA and serum, established to investigate the genetic epidemiology of melanoma.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>WAMHS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Western Australian Melanoma Health Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001432"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST010304</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association meta-analyses combining multiple risk phenotypes provide insights into the genetic architecture of cutaneous melanoma susceptibility.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32341527</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41588-020-0611-8/MediaObjects/41588_2020_611_MOESM1_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The WAMHS population based study was collected from Perth, Australia based on histopathology confirmed invasive, cutaneous melanoma cases in the Western Australian Cancer Registry.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001432"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based collection of adult melanoma cases resident in Western Australia and diagnosed between January 2006 and September 2009, identified through the Western Australian Cancer Registry and invited by mail with their diagnosing doctor&apos;s consent, with registry pathology data, a telephone questionnaire on melanoma risk factors such as sun exposure and skin type, and blood samples for DNA, RNA and serum, established to investigate the genetic epidemiology of melanoma.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:21474410</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:26237428</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001432"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>WAMHS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001432"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41588-020-0611-8/MediaObjects/41588_2020_611_MOESM1_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The WAMHS population based study was collected from Perth, Australia based on histopathology confirmed invasive, cutaneous melanoma cases in the Western Australian Cancer Registry.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001433 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001433">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Africa"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Eastern_Africa"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/South_Africa"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Southern_Africa"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Uganda"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90454283</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An observational study of adults taking warfarin at a stable maintenance dose for venous thromboembolism, atrial fibrillation or valvular heart disease, Black African and, in South Africa, also of mixed ancestry, recruited from June 2018 to March 2020 at outpatient clinics and hospital departments in Uganda and South Africa by the NIHR Global Health Research Group on warfarin anticoagulation in sub-Saharan Africa (the University of Liverpool, the Infectious Diseases Institute in Kampala and the University of Cape Town), with clinical data and blood samples for genotyping, to develop clinical and genetic warfarin dosing algorithms for sub-Saharan African patients.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>WARfarin anticoagulation in PATients in Sub-Saharan Africa</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>War-PATH</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>WarPATH</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Warfarin Anticoagulation in Patients in Sub-Saharan Africa</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Warfarin Anticoagulation in Patients in sub-Saharan Africa</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001433"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90454283</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies of stable warfarin dose in patients of African ancestry.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39163621</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11493193/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The African cohorts were obtained through the Warfarin Anticoagulation in Patients in sub-Saharan Africa (War-PATH http://warpath.info/) observational study.16, 17, 18 The War-PATH study enrolled participants receiving warfarin treatment, who were on stable dose, defined as consistent prescribed doses for 2 consecutive clinic visits in the year before recruitment.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001433"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An observational study of adults taking warfarin at a stable maintenance dose for venous thromboembolism, atrial fibrillation or valvular heart disease, Black African and, in South Africa, also of mixed ancestry, recruited from June 2018 to March 2020 at outpatient clinics and hospital departments in Uganda and South Africa by the NIHR Global Health Research Group on warfarin anticoagulation in sub-Saharan Africa (the University of Liverpool, the Infectious Diseases Institute in Kampala and the University of Cape Town), with clinical data and blood samples for genotyping, to develop clinical and genetic warfarin dosing algorithms for sub-Saharan African patients.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:36210801</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:39163621</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT03512080</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://warpath.info/</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
