    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001494 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001494">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Denmark"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:21104233</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A case–control study of osteoporosis at Aarhus University Hospital, Denmark, whose cases are osteoporotic men and women with vertebral fractures and low bone mineral density and whose controls are normal individuals, assessed with dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry, radiographs and bone turnover markers and genotyped for candidate-gene studies and for replication of genome-wide association findings on bone mineral density and fracture. It took part in the GENOMOS consortium and in the GEFOS meta-analysis of bone mineral density and fracture.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AROS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Aarhus Osteoporosis Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001494"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:21104233</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Polymorphisms in the ALOX12 gene and osteoporosis.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21104233</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21104233+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>We examined the effect of ALOX12 polymorphisms on BMD and the risk of fractures in two Danish cohorts: AROS, a case-control population comprising 809 individuals and DOPS, a population comprising 1,716 perimenopausal women allocated to hormone therapy or not at baseline and followed for up to 10 years.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001494"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A case–control study of osteoporosis at Aarhus University Hospital, Denmark, whose cases are osteoporotic men and women with vertebral fractures and low bone mineral density and whose controls are normal individuals, assessed with dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry, radiographs and bone turnover markers and genotyped for candidate-gene studies and for replication of genome-wide association findings on bone mineral density and fracture. It took part in the GENOMOS consortium and in the GEFOS meta-analysis of bone mineral density and fracture.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:16475872</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:17205326</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:22504420</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:24516880</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>http://www.gefos.org/?q=content/aarhus-osteoporosis-study-0</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001494"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>AROS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001494"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Denmark"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21104233+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>We examined the effect of ALOX12 polymorphisms on BMD and the risk of fractures in two Danish cohorts: AROS, a case-control population comprising 809 individuals and DOPS, a population comprising 1,716 perimenopausal women allocated to hormone therapy or not at baseline and followed for up to 10 years.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001495 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001495">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Netherlands"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:39462804</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An ongoing prospective, community-based registry, set up in June 2005 and run from the Academic Medical Center in Amsterdam, of all out-of-hospital cardiac arrests with a resuscitation attempt by emergency medical services in the province of North Holland, the Netherlands, which collects resuscitation and ECG data, outcomes, medical history from hospitals and general practitioners, medication use from community pharmacies and, since July 2007, DNA from patients with documented ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. It studies the outcome and the clinical, pharmacological, environmental and genetic determinants of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, taking controls from pharmacy and general-practice databases for case–control analyses.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ARREST</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AmsteRdam REsuscitation STudies</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>AmsteRdam Resuscitation Studies</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Amsterdam Resuscitation Studies</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001495"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:39462804</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Association Between Delay to First Shock and Successful First-Shock Ventricular Fibrillation Termination in Patients With Witnessed Out-of-Hospital Cardiac Arrest.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39462804</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A39462804+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Patients with a witnessed out-of-hospital cardiac arrest and ventricular fibrillation (VF) as an initial rhythm were included using the prospective ARREST registry (Amsterdam Resuscitation Studies).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001495"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An ongoing prospective, community-based registry, set up in June 2005 and run from the Academic Medical Center in Amsterdam, of all out-of-hospital cardiac arrests with a resuscitation attempt by emergency medical services in the province of North Holland, the Netherlands, which collects resuscitation and ECG data, outcomes, medical history from hospitals and general practitioners, medication use from community pharmacies and, since July 2007, DNA from patients with documented ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. It studies the outcome and the clinical, pharmacological, environmental and genetic determinants of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, taking controls from pharmacy and general-practice databases for case–control analyses.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:25332818</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://pure.amsterdamumc.nl/en/datasets/arrest-amsterdam-resuscitation-studies/</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001495"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ARREST</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001495"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Netherlands"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3419243/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>ARREST is a prospective, community-based study aimed at establishing the genetic and clinical determinants of SCA in the population of a contiguous region (urban and rural communities, ∼2.4 million inhabitants) of the Netherlands.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001496 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001496">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000001"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:19734902</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A collaboration of United Kingdom Alzheimer&apos;s disease research groups at the Universities of Nottingham, Manchester, Southampton and Bristol, Queen&apos;s University Belfast and the Oxford Project to Investigate Memory and Ageing, funded by the Alzheimer&apos;s Research Trust (renamed Alzheimer&apos;s Research UK, after which the collaboration is called the ARUK Consortium), that pooled DNA from people with probable or post-mortem-confirmed Alzheimer&apos;s disease and from elderly controls into a DNA bank hosted at the University of Nottingham. Its cases and controls formed one of the sample collections of the GERAD genome-wide association study of Alzheimer&apos;s disease.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ART</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ART Collaboration</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ARUK Consortium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Alzheimer&apos;s Research UK Consortium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Alzheimer&apos;s Research Trust Collaboration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001496"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:19734902</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association study identifies variants at CLU and PICALM associated with Alzheimer&apos;s disease.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:19734902</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.440/MediaObjects/41588_2009_BFng440_MOESM9_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Samples were drawn from the MRC genetic resource for AD; the ART Collaboration; Competence Network of Dementia and Department of Psychiatry, University of Bonn; Aristotle University of Thessaloniki</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001496"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A collaboration of United Kingdom Alzheimer&apos;s disease research groups at the Universities of Nottingham, Manchester, Southampton and Bristol, Queen&apos;s University Belfast and the Oxford Project to Investigate Memory and Ageing, funded by the Alzheimer&apos;s Research Trust (renamed Alzheimer&apos;s Research UK, after which the collaboration is called the ARUK Consortium), that pooled DNA from people with probable or post-mortem-confirmed Alzheimer&apos;s disease and from elderly controls into a DNA bank hosted at the University of Nottingham. Its cases and controls formed one of the sample collections of the GERAD genome-wide association study of Alzheimer&apos;s disease.</owl:annotatedTarget>
