    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001497 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001497">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Hungary"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Romania"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Slovakia"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:22436128</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A hospital-based case–control study of basal cell carcinoma of the skin and of bladder and kidney cancers in relation to low-level inorganic arsenic in drinking water, funded by the European Commission and conducted in 2002–2004 in counties of Hungary, Romania and Slovakia with arsenic in groundwater. Its incident cases, and its controls drawn from general surgery, orthopaedic and trauma in-patients of hospitals across the same areas and frequency matched to the cases by age, sex and county, all aged 30–79, were assessed for lifetime arsenic exposure, urinary arsenic metabolites and genetic susceptibility.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ASHRAM</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Arsenic Health Risk Assessment and Molecular Epidemiology study in Central Europe</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Arsenic Health Risk Assessment and Molecular Epidemiology</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001497"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:22436128</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Inorganic arsenic and basal cell carcinoma in areas of Hungary, Romania, and Slovakia: a case-control study.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:22436128</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A22436128+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Arsenic Health Risk Assessment and Molecular Epidemiology (ASHRAM) study, a case-control study, was conducted in areas of Hungary, Romania, and Slovakia with reported presence of iAs in groundwater.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001497"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A hospital-based case–control study of basal cell carcinoma of the skin and of bladder and kidney cancers in relation to low-level inorganic arsenic in drinking water, funded by the European Commission and conducted in 2002–2004 in counties of Hungary, Romania and Slovakia with arsenic in groundwater. Its incident cases, and its controls drawn from general surgery, orthopaedic and trauma in-patients of hospitals across the same areas and frequency matched to the cases by age, sex and county, all aged 30–79, were assessed for lifetime arsenic exposure, urinary arsenic metabolites and genetic susceptibility.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:20401490</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:21131975</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:22436128</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001497"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ASHRAM</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001497"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Slovakia"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A20401490+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Methods and results are presented for an arsenic exposure assessment integral to an epidemiological case-control study of arsenic and cancer-the European Commission funded ASHRAM (Arsenic Health Risk Assessment and Molecular Epidemiology) study carried out in some counties of Hungary, Romania and Slovakia.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001498 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001498">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Greece"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:15279051</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A study of young men aged 18–24 doing compulsory military service in Greece, randomly selected in successive waves during their first two weeks at the National Basic Air Force Training Center in Tripolis, who completed self-rated questionnaires on schizotypy and psychopathology and computerised neurocognitive and eye-movement tasks and gave DNA, a subgroup being reassessed at discharge two years later. Led from the University Mental Health Research Institute in Athens, it studies psychosis proneness and its cognitive and genetic correlates in the general population.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ASPIS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Athens Study of Psychosis Proneness and Incidence of Schizophrenia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001498"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:15279051</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Factorial composition of self-rated schizotypal traits among young males undergoing military training.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:15279051</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A15279051+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The aim of this study within the Athens Study of Psychosis Proneness and Incidence of Schizophrenia (ASPIS) was the examination of the latent structure of schizotypal dimensions among a large population of young male conscripts in the Greek Air Force during their first week of military training.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001498"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A study of young men aged 18–24 doing compulsory military service in Greece, randomly selected in successive waves during their first two weeks at the National Basic Air Force Training Center in Tripolis, who completed self-rated questionnaires on schizotypy and psychopathology and computerised neurocognitive and eye-movement tasks and gave DNA, a subgroup being reassessed at discharge two years later. Led from the University Mental Health Research Institute in Athens, it studies psychosis proneness and its cognitive and genetic correlates in the general population.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:18834502</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:22503357</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23155182</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001498"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>ASPIS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001498"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Greece"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A15279051+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The aim of this study within the Athens Study of Psychosis Proneness and Incidence of Schizophrenia (ASPIS) was the examination of the latent structure of schizotypal dimensions among a large population of young male conscripts in the Greek Air Force during their first week of military training.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001499 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001499">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000001"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Oceania"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:23599335</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An Australian twin study of eye traits that combines twins, and some of their siblings, given an eye examination in the Twins Eye Study in Tasmania or in the Brisbane Adolescent Twin Study, with measures such as cycloplegic refraction, intraocular pressure and retinal photographs and with genome-wide genotypes, used in heritability, linkage and genome-wide association studies of ophthalmic traits. Its participants were recruited through the Australian Twin Registry, existing twin studies, schools and publicity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>ATES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Australian Twin Eye study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Australian Twin Eye Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001499"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:23599335</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Copy number variation at chromosome 5q21.2 is associated with intraocular pressure.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23599335</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23599335+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Australian Twin Eye Study and BMES both replicated the 5q21.2 CNV association and direction of effect (P = 0.001 and P = 0.02, respectively).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001499"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An Australian twin study of eye traits that combines twins, and some of their siblings, given an eye examination in the Twins Eye Study in Tasmania or in the Brisbane Adolescent Twin Study, with measures such as cycloplegic refraction, intraocular pressure and retinal photographs and with genome-wide genotypes, used in heritability, linkage and genome-wide association studies of ophthalmic traits. Its participants were recruited through the Australian Twin Registry, existing twin studies, schools and publicity.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:19803772</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:20835236</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23116649</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23599335</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
