    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001524 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001524">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Bangladesh"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/South_Asia"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:25133637</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based, 2×2 factorial, double-blind randomised controlled chemoprevention trial in Bangladesh that enrolled adults with manifest arsenical skin lesions from the Narayanganj, Comilla, Noakhali and Chandpur districts, through field offices in Araihazar and Matlab, between April 2006 and August 2009, and gave them vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol), selenium (L-selenomethionine), both or placebo for six years to test the prevention of non-melanoma skin cancer, with all-cause and cancer mortality, diabetes and oxidative stress biomarkers as secondary endpoints.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">The acronym BEST also denotes Black Women with Breast Cancer: Etiology, Survival, and Treatment Outcomes Study (COHO:0001525); this term is Bangladesh Vitamin E and Selenium Trial.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BEST</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Bangladesh Vitamin E and Selenium Trial</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001524"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:25133637</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association studies and heritability estimates of body mass index related phenotypes in Bangladeshi adults.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25133637</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4136799/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Bangladesh Vitamin E and Selenium Trial (BEST) [28] was established in Araihazar and surrounding areas in 2006 to determine whether 7,000 arsenic-exposed participants who supplemented their diet with vitamin E and selenium would reduce their rate of non-melanoma skin cancer.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001524"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based, 2×2 factorial, double-blind randomised controlled chemoprevention trial in Bangladesh that enrolled adults with manifest arsenical skin lesions from the Narayanganj, Comilla, Noakhali and Chandpur districts, through field offices in Araihazar and Matlab, between April 2006 and August 2009, and gave them vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol), selenium (L-selenomethionine), both or placebo for six years to test the prevention of non-melanoma skin cancer, with all-cause and cancer mortality, diabetes and oxidative stress biomarkers as secondary endpoints.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23590571</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001524"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BEST</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001524"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Bangladesh"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23590571+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>This study reports on the design, methods and baseline analyses from the Bangladesh Vitamin E and Selenium Trial (BEST), a population-based chemoprevention study conducted among adults in Bangladesh with visible arsenic toxicity.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001525 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001525">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:41629673</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based longitudinal cohort of self-identified Black women diagnosed with invasive breast cancer at age 50 or younger, identified through the state cancer registries of Florida and Tennessee (diagnoses from 2005 to 2018; its first phase recruited women diagnosed in Florida in 2009–2012), who completed a questionnaire on sociodemographic, reproductive, epidemiologic and lifestyle factors, released their medical records and tumour tissue and gave saliva for germline genetic testing, now based at Vanderbilt University Medical Center, to study the causes, inherited predisposition, treatment and survival of breast cancer, including triple-negative breast cancer, in young Black women.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">The acronym BEST also denotes Bangladesh Vitamin E and Selenium Trial (COHO:0001524); this term is Black Women with Breast Cancer: Etiology, Survival, and Treatment Outcomes Study.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BEST</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BEST study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BEST_Florida</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Black Women: Etiology and Survival of Triple-Negative Breast Cancer study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Black Women: Etiology and Survival of Triple-negative Breast Cancers (BEST) Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Black Women: Etiology and Survival of Triple-negative Breast Cancers Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>The Black Women: Etiology and Survival of Triple-Negative Breast Cancers (BEST) Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Black Women with Breast Cancer: Etiology, Survival, and Treatment Outcomes Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001525"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:41629673</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Improved polygenic risk prediction models for breast cancer subtypes in women of African ancestry.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:41629673</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://europepmc.org/api/getPdf?pmcid=PMC13007722</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>We additionally performed external validation of PRS models for each of the four BC phenotypes in an independent combined dataset of women of African ancestry (580 cases and 3,011 controls) from three studies of women of African ancestry including The Vanderbilt Biobank (BioVU),42,43 Black Women: Etiology and Survival of Triple-negative Breast Cancers (BEST) Study,44 and the Women Firefighters Biomonitoring Collaborative (WFBC).45</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001525"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based longitudinal cohort of self-identified Black women diagnosed with invasive breast cancer at age 50 or younger, identified through the state cancer registries of Florida and Tennessee (diagnoses from 2005 to 2018; its first phase recruited women diagnosed in Florida in 2009–2012), who completed a questionnaire on sociodemographic, reproductive, epidemiologic and lifestyle factors, released their medical records and tumour tissue and gave saliva for germline genetic testing, now based at Vanderbilt University Medical Center, to study the causes, inherited predisposition, treatment and survival of breast cancer, including triple-negative breast cancer, in young Black women.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:26287763</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:33947654</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:41360000</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:42252839</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
