    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:21249187</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A case-control genetic study of bipolar disorder in the United States, initiated in 2006 as the genetic component of the NIMH-sponsored Genetics Initiative for Bipolar Disorder Consortium to carry out genome-wide association studies: cases with bipolar disorder of European and African American ancestry, collected through the consortium&apos;s eleven US collection sites (mostly from multiplex families or sibling pairs for the first, GAIN-funded genotyping; without regard to family history for the later TGen genotyping), and controls from the NIMH Genetics Initiative control sample collected for the Molecular Genetics of Schizophrenia study.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BIGS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BiGS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Bipolar Genome Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:21249187</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Accuracy of CNV Detection from GWAS Data.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21249187</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21249187+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The second evaluation was program performance calling rare and common CNVs in the Bipolar Genome Study (BiGS) data set (1001 bipolar cases and 1033 controls, all of European ancestry) as measured by the Affymetrix SNP 6.0 array.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A case-control genetic study of bipolar disorder in the United States, initiated in 2006 as the genetic component of the NIMH-sponsored Genetics Initiative for Bipolar Disorder Consortium to carry out genome-wide association studies: cases with bipolar disorder of European and African American ancestry, collected through the consortium&apos;s eleven US collection sites (mostly from multiplex families or sibling pairs for the first, GAIN-funded genotyping; without regard to family history for the later TGen genotyping), and controls from the NIMH Genetics Initiative control sample collected for the Molecular Genetics of Schizophrenia study.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:19488044</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:21738484</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BIGS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BiGS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001532"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21595007+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Participants in the US Bipolar Genome Study (BIGS) were assessed on six items of concerns in the Questionnaire on Genetic Risk (QGR).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001533 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001533">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Lebanon"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:37933522</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A longitudinal cohort of Syrian refugee children aged 8–16 and their primary caregivers, from families who had fled the war in Syria within the previous four years and were living in informal tented settlements in the West and Central Beqaa regions of Lebanon, recruited by purposive cluster sampling from October 2017 to January 2018 and followed up once a year later, with interviews of children and caregivers and children&apos;s saliva (for genotyping and DNA methylation) and hair (for cortisol, DHEA and testosterone), to study how psychosocial and biological factors shape resilience and mental health problems; it was funded by the US National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and sponsored by Queen Mary University of London.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BIOPATH</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Biological Pathways of Risk and Resilience in Syrian Refugee Children</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Biological pathways of risk and resilience in Syrian refugee children</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001533"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:37933522</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Risk and resilience in Syrian refugee children: A multisystem analysis.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:37933522</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/development-and-psychopathology/article/risk-and-resilience-in-syrian-refugee-children-a-multisystem-analysis/C219D707EC55AA6D51903AA8E512C875</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Participants were drawn from the biological pathways of risk and resilience in Syrian refugee children (BIOPATH) study (McEwen et al., Reference McEwen, Popham, Moghames, Smeeth, de Villiers, Saab, Karam, Fayyad, Karam and Pluess2022).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001533"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A longitudinal cohort of Syrian refugee children aged 8–16 and their primary caregivers, from families who had fled the war in Syria within the previous four years and were living in informal tented settlements in the West and Central Beqaa regions of Lebanon, recruited by purposive cluster sampling from October 2017 to January 2018 and followed up once a year later, with interviews of children and caregivers and children&apos;s saliva (for genotyping and DNA methylation) and hair (for cortisol, DHEA and testosterone), to study how psychosocial and biological factors shape resilience and mental health problems; it was funded by the US National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and sponsored by Queen Mary University of London.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:35041012</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:37933522</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001533"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BIOPATH</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001533"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Lebanon"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A35041012+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Based in Lebanon, a middle-income country significantly impacted by the refugee crisis, it is the first such cohort of refugees in the Middle East.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001534 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001534">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Belgium"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Canada"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Eastern_Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Hong_Kong"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Oceania"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sweden"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:34197840</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An international observational cohort study, led from the University of Washington, of adults with kidney failure treated with peritoneal dialysis who had a peritoneal equilibration test soon after starting it, set up to find common genetic variants that influence peritoneal solute transfer rate and ultrafiltration. Participants were enrolled prospectively at centres in Australia, Canada, the United Kingdom and the United States from 2013 to 2018, and DNA and clinical data were added from earlier peritoneal dialysis studies in the United Kingdom (PD-CRAFT), Belgium, Sweden and Hong Kong.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Bio-PD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Biological Determinants of PD</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Biological Determinants of Peritoneal Dialysis (Bio-PD) study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Biological Determinants of Peritoneal Dialysis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001534"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:34197840</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>A genome-wide association study suggests correlations of common genetic variants with peritoneal solute transfer rates in patients with kidney failure receiving peritoneal dialysis.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:34197840</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC8545920/unicode</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To bridge this gap in knowledge, we undertook the Biological Determinants of PD (Bio-PD) study to perform a GWAS to test the hypothesis that common genetic variants explain some of the inter-individual variability in PSTR in people with kidney failure treated with PD.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001534"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An international observational cohort study, led from the University of Washington, of adults with kidney failure treated with peritoneal dialysis who had a peritoneal equilibration test soon after starting it, set up to find common genetic variants that influence peritoneal solute transfer rate and ultrafiltration. Participants were enrolled prospectively at centres in Australia, Canada, the United Kingdom and the United States from 2013 to 2018, and DNA and clinical data were added from earlier peritoneal dialysis studies in the United Kingdom (PD-CRAFT), Belgium, Sweden and Hong Kong.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:34197840</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:40669005</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies/NCT02694068</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001534"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Bio-PD</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001534"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12807162/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Bio-PD study enrolled 3817 participants undergoing PD at 70 centers across Australia, Belgium, Canada, Hong Kong, Sweden, the United Kingdom, and the United States.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/France"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Germany"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Greece"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Italy"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Netherlands"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Norway"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Poland"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Serbia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sweden"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:31919934</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multicentre, prospective, observational European study of adults with new-onset or worsening heart failure who were treated with loop diuretics but received no more than half the target doses of ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers and/or beta-blockers, and whose physicians intended to start or up-titrate these drugs. Its index cohort was enrolled at 69 centres in 11 European countries between December 2010 and December 2012 and its validation cohort at six centres in Scotland between October 2010 and April 2014; funded by the European Commission&apos;s Seventh Framework Programme, it combined clinical data, biomarkers, genome-wide genotyping and proteomics to find patient profiles that predict response to guideline-recommended therapy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>A Systems Biology Study to Tailored Treatment in Chronic Heart Failure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BIOSTAT-CHF</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BIOlogy Study to TAilored Treatment in Chronic Heart Failure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Systems Biology Study to Tailored Treatment in Chronic Heart Failure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">A systems BIOlogy Study to TAilored Treatment in Chronic Heart Failure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:31919934</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genetic risk and atrial fibrillation in patients with heart failure.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:31919934</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A31919934+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>An AF genetic risk score was calculated in 3759 European ancestry individuals (1783 with sinus rhythm, 1976 with AF) from the BIOlogy Study to TAilored Treatment in Chronic Heart Failure (BIOSTAT-CHF) by summing 97 single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) alleles (ranging from 0-2) weighted by the natural logarithm of the relative SNP risk from the latest AF genome-wide association study.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A multicentre, prospective, observational European study of adults with new-onset or worsening heart failure who were treated with loop diuretics but received no more than half the target doses of ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers and/or beta-blockers, and whose physicians intended to start or up-titrate these drugs. Its index cohort was enrolled at 69 centres in 11 European countries between December 2010 and December 2012 and its validation cohort at six centres in Scotland between October 2010 and April 2014; funded by the European Commission&apos;s Seventh Framework Programme, it combined clinical data, biomarkers, genome-wide genotyping and proteomics to find patient profiles that predict response to guideline-recommended therapy.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:27126231</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:31919934</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:32002631</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:32825781</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:33998162</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>BIOSTAT-CHF</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Poland"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7551091/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Country Number of Included Patients Mean Selenium Percentage of Patients &lt; 100 μg/L the Netherlands 386 86.00 76.68 France 243 80.70 80.66 Germancy 87 90.69 67.82 Serbia 382 91.23 66.49 Slovenia 43 66.83 97.67 Greece 285 90.87 68.42 Italy 297 115.48 27.61 Norway 108 79.16 86.11 Sweden 97 79.49 84.54 Poland 239 79.33 87.45 United Kingdom 161 84.42 77.64</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001535"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8318449/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>In brief, BIOSTAT‐CHF was a multicentre, prospective, observational study that included both an index cohort of 2516 chronic HF patients with worsening signs and/or symptoms from 11 European countries, who were considered to be on suboptimal medical treatment, and a validation cohort of 1738 HF patients was recruited from six centres in Scotland.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001536 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001536">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:23717212</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An unmatched case-control study of severe Plasmodium falciparum malaria at Queen Elizabeth Central Hospital, Blantyre, Malawi, run by the Malawi-Liverpool Wellcome Trust Clinical Research Programme with the Blantyre Malaria Project. Its cases are children aged 2 months to 14 years admitted with severe malaria (cerebral malaria or severe malarial anaemia) in 1994-2007, its controls are cord-blood samples from infants born at the same hospital in 2006-2007, and blood was also taken from some cases&apos; parents; it is the Malawi partner study of the MalariaGEN Consortial Project 1 and its genome-wide association studies of severe malaria.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Blantyre</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Blantyre-Wellcome Trust Project in Blantyre, Malawi</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Human genetic determinants of severe malaria in Malawi</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001536"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:23717212</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Imputation-based meta-analysis of severe malaria in three African populations.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23717212</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3662650/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Here we describe partner projects from the MRC laboratories in Fajara, Gambia, The KEMRI-Wellcome Trust Unit in Kilif, Kenya and The Blantyre-Wellcome Trust Project in Blantyre, Malawi (Table S1).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001536"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An unmatched case-control study of severe Plasmodium falciparum malaria at Queen Elizabeth Central Hospital, Blantyre, Malawi, run by the Malawi-Liverpool Wellcome Trust Clinical Research Programme with the Blantyre Malaria Project. Its cases are children aged 2 months to 14 years admitted with severe malaria (cerebral malaria or severe malarial anaemia) in 1994-2007, its controls are cord-blood samples from infants born at the same hospital in 2006-2007, and blood was also taken from some cases&apos; parents; it is the Malawi partner study of the MalariaGEN Consortial Project 1 and its genome-wide association studies of severe malaria.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23717212</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.malariagen.net/partner_study/human-genetic-determinants-severe-malaria-malawi/</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001536"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Blantyre</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001537 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001537">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Germany"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:29574091</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An ongoing, prospective, multicentre diagnostic study of participants in the German screening colonoscopy programme, recruited since November 2005 at a preparatory visit before colonoscopy at gastroenterology practices in southern Germany, who donate blood and stool samples and answer a questionnaire. Run from the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) in Heidelberg, it evaluates new blood and stool tests for the early detection of colorectal cancer and advanced adenomas against the findings of screening colonoscopy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BLITZ</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>BliTz</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Blitz</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Evaluation of novel markers for early detection of colorectal cancer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Begleitende Evaluierung innovativer Testverfahren zur Darmkrebsfrüherkennung</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001537"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:29574091</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genetic Risk Score Is Associated With Prevalence of Advanced Neoplasms in a Colorectal Cancer Screening Population.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:29574091</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC6035076/unicode</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Data for the analyses were drawn from the BLITZ study (Begleitende Evaluierung innovativer Testverfahren zur Darmkrebsfrüherkennung) which has been described in detail before.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001537"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An ongoing, prospective, multicentre diagnostic study of participants in the German screening colonoscopy programme, recruited since November 2005 at a preparatory visit before colonoscopy at gastroenterology practices in southern Germany, who donate blood and stool samples and answer a questionnaire. Run from the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) in Heidelberg, it evaluates new blood and stool tests for the early detection of colorectal cancer and advanced adenomas against the findings of screening colonoscopy.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:29574091</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:35060941</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:38704818</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://drks.de/search/en/trial/DRKS00008737</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
