    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001608"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Denmark"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A42604063+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Danish National Patient Register (DNPR) is an important data source for register-based health-related research, providing detailed information on all hospital contacts in Denmark.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001609 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001609">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:23151390</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A study of young adult university students aged 18–22, most of them Duke University undergraduates recruited on campus in Durham, North Carolina, United States, who between 2010 and 2016 completed self-report and behavioural measures, functional and structural brain MRI and genotyping in Duke University&apos;s Laboratory of NeuroGenetics. People with psychiatric diagnoses were not excluded, as the study set out to relate variation in behaviour, brain and genes to risk for psychopathology.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DNS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Duke Neurogenetics study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Duke Neurogenetics Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001609"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:23151390</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Neural responses to threat and reward interact to predict stress-related problem drinking: A novel protective role of the amygdala.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23151390</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A23151390+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>We used well-established neuroimaging paradigms to probe threat-related amygdala and reward-related ventral striatum reactivity in a sample of 200 young adult students from the ongoing Duke Neurogenetics Study.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001609"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A study of young adult university students aged 18–22, most of them Duke University undergraduates recruited on campus in Durham, North Carolina, United States, who between 2010 and 2016 completed self-report and behavioural measures, functional and structural brain MRI and genotyping in Duke University&apos;s Laboratory of NeuroGenetics. People with psychiatric diagnoses were not excluded, as the study set out to relate variation in behaviour, brain and genes to risk for psychopathology.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:28887539</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:31629936</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:31677585</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:38711811</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001609"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DNS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001609"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11071117/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Duke Neurogenetics Study (DNS) data were collected from high-functioning young adult university students in the United States, so that it would be premature to assume that the same findings would be replicated in samples drawn from the general population.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001610 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001610">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Oceania"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:27649491</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A prospective population-based study of osteoporosis and fracture that in 1989 invited all men and women aged 60 or over living in Dubbo, New South Wales, Australia, a city chosen because its age and sex structure resembled Australia&apos;s and its isolation allowed near-complete ascertainment of fractures, and recruited further participants from 2000. Run by investigators of the Garvan Institute of Medical Research, Sydney, it reassessed participants every two to three years with interviews and bone densitometry and recorded fractures from the area&apos;s radiology services until 2020.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DOES</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Dubbo Osteoporosis Epidemiology study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Dubbo Osteoporosis Epidemiology Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001610"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:27649491</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Prediction of Bone Mineral Density and Fragility Fracture by Genetic Profiling.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:27649491</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A27649491+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Sixty-two bone mineral density (BMD)-associated single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) were genotyped in 557 men and 902 women who had participated in the Dubbo Osteoporosis Epidemiology Study.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001610"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A prospective population-based study of osteoporosis and fracture that in 1989 invited all men and women aged 60 or over living in Dubbo, New South Wales, Australia, a city chosen because its age and sex structure resembled Australia&apos;s and its isolation allowed near-complete ascertainment of fractures, and recruited further participants from 2000. Run by investigators of the Garvan Institute of Medical Research, Sydney, it reassessed participants every two to three years with interviews and bone densitometry and recorded fractures from the area&apos;s radiology services until 2020.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:15727686</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:37701154</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:38485241</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001610"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DOES</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001610"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A7812076+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>This longitudinal population-based study documents the incidence of all symptomatic fractures from 1989 to 1992 in an elderly, predominantly Caucasian population of males and females (&gt; or = 60 years as at 1 January 1989) living in the geographically isolated region of the city of Dubbo, NSW, Australia.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001611 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001611">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Denmark"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:10340280</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An investigator-initiated, partly randomised multicentre trial in Denmark that in 1990–1993 enrolled healthy white women aged 45–58 in early menopause, recruited by direct mailing to a random sample of women from the general population, and allocated them, open label, to hormone replacement therapy or no treatment either at random or by their own choice. Planned to run for 20 years, with its randomised treatment stopped in 2002, it was set up to test whether hormone replacement therapy started soon after menopause prevents osteoporotic fractures and to find clinical, biochemical and densitometric predictors of low bone mass and fracture.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DOPS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Danish Osteoporosis Prevention Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001611"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:10340280</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>The Danish Osteoporosis Prevention Study (DOPS): project design and inclusion of 2000 normal perimenopausal women.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:10340280</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A10340280+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>In 1990 we initiated a 20 year, partly randomised study (Danish Osteoporosis Prevention Study, DOPS) in order to (a) evaluate clinical, biochemical and osteodensitometric variables as predictors of low bone mass and future osteoporotic fractures, and (b) test the hypothesis, that hormone replacement therapy (HRT) initiated shortly after menopause reduces the risk of later osteoporotic fractures.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001611"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An investigator-initiated, partly randomised multicentre trial in Denmark that in 1990–1993 enrolled healthy white women aged 45–58 in early menopause, recruited by direct mailing to a random sample of women from the general population, and allocated them, open label, to hormone replacement therapy or no treatment either at random or by their own choice. Planned to run for 20 years, with its randomised treatment stopped in 2002, it was set up to test whether hormone replacement therapy started soon after menopause prevents osteoporotic fractures and to find clinical, biochemical and densitometric predictors of low bone mass and fracture.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:10340280</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23048011</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001611"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>DOPS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:42365137</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region&apos;s SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DOV</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>DOVE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Diseases of the Ovary and Their Evaluation Study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Diseases of the Ovary and their Evaluation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Diseases of the Ovary and their Evaluation Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:42365137</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Ovarian cancer tumor immune profiles associated with intrauterine device and oral contraceptive use.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:42365137</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13534713/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>This analysis included women with epithelial ovarian cancer from six studies: African American Cancer Epidemiology Study (AACES), Diseases of the Ovary and their Evaluation Study (DOVE), Hormones and Ovarian Cancer Prediction Study (HOPE), New England Case Control Study (NECC), Nurses’ Health Study (NHS), and NHSII.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based case–control study of epithelial ovarian cancer in western Washington State, United States, run from Seattle with funding from the US National Cancer Institute, that enrolled women aged 35–74 diagnosed in 2002–2009 in a 13-county area, identified through the region&apos;s SEER cancer registry, together with controls from the same area identified by random-digit dialling, and collected in-person interviews and, from cases, tumour tissue.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:19127255</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:19240718</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23065074</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:36826816</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:42365137</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001612"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
